VW("hourly","pipeline","monitors","Hourly monitors",{s:"ok",nrs:["hourly-pipeline"],eyebrow:"Hourly grain · pipeline",sub:"Poisson or negative binomial",lede:"An hourly count varies around the day's level, and the level itself varies (about 11 % beyond Poisson here). Limits that ignore the second raise alarms on normal hours.",src:"granularity_time 02",blocks:[
 S("False-alarm rate of an hourly monitor","Set the expected events per hour and the day-level variation; compare Poisson limits with negative-binomial ones under the same truth.",
  '<div class="calc" id="calcNb"><div class="row"><label class="f">expected events per hour<input type="number" id="nbMu" value="56" min="1" max="5000"></label><label class="f">day-level variation (CV, %)<input type="number" id="nbCv" value="11" min="0" max="60" step="1"></label><label class="f">target alarm rate (%)<input type="number" id="nbA" value="0.1" min="0.001" max="10" step="0.01"></label></div><div class="out" id="nbOut"></div></div>'),
 {html:P("concept",["Concept","Why the negative binomial"],'<p>If each hour is Poisson with a mean that varies from day to day (a gamma-distributed level), the count is negative binomial with variance μ + (CV·μ)². At small μ both limits agree; at large μ the level variation dominates and Poisson limits become far too narrow. 56 events is the average market-hour; a market × channel hour averages about 9.</p>',true)}]});
